---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'install', label: '1. Install the CLI' },
   { id: 'check', label: '2. Check your machine' },
   { id: 'audit', label: '3. Run an accessibility audit' },
   { id: 'session', label: '4. Start a virtual screen reader' },
   { id: 'navigate', label: '5. Drive keyboard navigation' },
   { id: 'stop', label: '6. Stop the session' },
   { id: 'next', label: 'Next steps' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Quickstart"
   summary="<p>Install <code>a11ied</code>, verify your local environment with <code>a1 doctor</code>, run an automated accessibility audit, and drive your first virtual <abbr title='Assistive technology software that speaks on-screen text and UI controls to blind and low-vision users'>screen reader</abbr> session in under two minutes.</p><p>The virtual screen reader runs in-process with zero extra system permissions or audio hardware required.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="install">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 1</p>
         <h2>Install the CLI</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Install <code>a11ied</code> globally, or add it to your project as a developer dependency:
      </p>
      <pre><code>npm install --global a11ied</code></pre>
      <p>
         The package provides two identical binaries: <code>a1</code> (short shorthand) and <code>a11ied</code>.
         Every example throughout these docs uses <code>a1</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         If you prefer not to install globally, run any command with <code>npx a11ied &lt;command&gt;</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="check">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 2</p>
         <h2>Check your environment</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Run <code>a1 doctor</code> to inspect your local setup. It checks for installed browsers,
         system permissions, and available screen reader targets:
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>✓ Ready for a1 commands on macOS 26.5.2 (arm64)

Targets
  ✓ macos-voiceover  ready  Ready for real screen reader sessions.
      ✓ AppleScript control enabled in VoiceOver settings
      ✓ AppleScript control confirmed by macOS
      ✓ VoiceOver local preferences exist
      ✓ VoiceOver welcome dialog suppressed
      ✓ Guidepup VoiceOver preferences bundle installed
      ✓ Screen recording available to the current host app
  - windows-nvda     Only available on Windows.
  ✓ virtual-dom      ready  No setup needed.

Browser automation
  ✓ Google Chrome  ready  Used for a1 axe scans.
      Path    /Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome
      Launch  system install, opened through the "chrome" browser channel
      Others  Playwright Chromium

  Policy    system-browser-first: an installed Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium is used before Playwright's Chromium
  Fallback  npx playwright install chromium

Environment
  a11ied  0.1.0
  Node    v24.19.0
  npm     11.17.0
  Host    macOS 26.5.2 (arm64) [darwin]</code></pre>
      <p>
         A leading <code>✓</code> indicates your system is ready. If a native tool needs configuration,
         <code>a1 doctor</code> displays an <strong>Action items</strong> list with the exact command to resolve it.
         Because the virtual screen reader used in this quickstart runs entirely in software, it requires zero
         OS configuration or elevated permissions.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="audit">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Step 3</p>
            <h2>Run an automated accessibility audit</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            Start your local development server, then scan a route with <code>a1 audit</code>.
            Powered by <abbr title="Open-source automated accessibility testing engine for HTML and DOM structure from Deque Systems"><a href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">axe-core</a></abbr>,
            it checks rendered DOM structure against official <abbr title="Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: the W3C standard of testable criteria for digital accessibility"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WCAG</a></abbr> success criteria:
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 audit http://localhost:3000/checkout --level AA</code></pre>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>✗ 1 problem to fix.

4.1.2  Name, Role, Value  [A]
  button[type="button"]
  Fix: Buttons must have discernible text. Add an aria-label or visible text.

Check by hand
  18 criteria this page triggers require interactive or screen reader review:
    • 2.1.1  Keyboard
    • 2.4.3  Focus Order
    • 4.1.3  Status Messages

The page
  Titled Checkout. It has 1 main landmark, 1 form, 2 textboxes, 2 buttons.</code></pre>
         <p>
            Every finding includes the official WCAG criterion, the failing element selector, and
            actionable remediation advice. When scripting in CI, pass <code>--json</code> for machine-readable output.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Static vs. runtime</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Notice the <strong>Check by hand</strong> section: static scanners catch DOM syntax errors,
            but cannot verify keyboard focus sequence, live region announcements, or widget state transitions.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="session">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Step 4</p>
            <h2>Start a virtual screen reader session</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            To verify what assistive technology actually announces when interacting with your interface,
            launch a virtual screen reader session against your URL:
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual --allow-virtual http://localhost:3000/checkout</code></pre>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>✓ Session drv_c33f1d8f4461 started
  Target: virtual (headless Chromium)
  URL:    http://localhost:3000/checkout</code></pre>
         <p>
            The virtual target runs inside a headless browser engine, computing the full <abbr title="Accessible Rich Internet Applications: W3C specification of attributes that define accessible UI roles, states, and properties"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ARIA</a></abbr> accessibility tree
            and speech queue. Page scripts, CSS transitions, and client-side framework components execute normally.
         </p>
         <p>
            On macOS and Windows, passing <code>--allow-virtual</code> acts as an explicit safeguard confirming you want
            the in-memory software engine rather than native <abbr title="Apple's built-in screen reader for macOS"><a href="https://support.apple.com/guide/voiceover/welcome/mac" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">VoiceOver</a></abbr> or <abbr title="NonVisual Desktop Access: the open-source screen reader for Windows"><a href="https://www.nvaccess.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NVDA</a></abbr>.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Active session</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Subsequent <code>a1 sr</code> commands automatically target this active session. You don't need
            to copy or manage session IDs in your terminal.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="navigate">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 5</p>
         <h2>Drive navigation and inspect speech</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Step through interactive elements one by one, or read the entire page from top to bottom
         with <code>read-all</code>:
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr read-all</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>document
heading, Checkout, level 1
form
Email
textbox, Email, required
Card number
textbox, Card number
button, Pay now
button
end of form
end of document</code></pre>
      <p>
         Review the transcript in chronological order:
      </p>
      <ul>
         <li>The first input correctly announces its label and its <strong>required</strong> state (satisfying <strong><abbr title="WCAG 4.1.2: Name, Role, Value requires that UI components have accessible names, roles, and states"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/name-role-value.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WCAG 4.1.2</a></abbr></strong>).</li>
         <li>The second button speaks only <code>button</code> with no accessible name—confirming the exact violation identified during the audit in Step 3.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
         You can also send individual keyboard strokes like <code>a1 sr press Tab</code>, <code>a1 sr press ArrowDown</code>,
         or <code>a1 sr next</code> to test dynamic widgets step-by-step.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="stop">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 6</p>
         <h2>Stop the session</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         When you're finished testing, stop the session to release worker processes and temporary state:
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr stop</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>✓ Session drv_c33f1d8f4461 stopped</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="next">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Next steps</p>
         <h2>Integrate accessibility into your workflow</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         You've just audited a page and verified screen reader announcements from your terminal. Here is how to take it further:
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Automate with Vitest</strong>
            <span>
               Run screen reader assertions alongside your unit tests using <code>a11ied/vitest</code>.
               See the <a href="/guides/vitest">Vitest guide</a>.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Equip AI coding agents</strong>
            <span>
               Connect the local <abbr title="Model Context Protocol: open standard for connecting AI coding assistants to external tools"><a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">MCP</a></abbr> server to Cursor, Claude Code, or Windsurf so agents can verify their own UI code.
               See the <a href="/guides/agents">AI agents guide</a>.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Test on real OS screen readers</strong>
            <span>
               Automate real macOS <abbr title="Apple's built-in screen reader for macOS"><a href="https://support.apple.com/guide/voiceover/welcome/mac" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">VoiceOver</a></abbr> or Windows <abbr title="NonVisual Desktop Access: the open-source screen reader for Windows"><a href="https://www.nvaccess.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NVDA</a></abbr> sessions and record video demos.
               See the <a href="/screen-readers">screen readers guide</a>.
            </span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>
</DocShell>
